msgid ""
"Complete reference for supported snap hooks, including lifecycle, "
"configuration, interface, refresh, installation, and removal hooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:8
msgid "Supported snap hooks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:10
msgid ""
"A hook is an executable file that runs within a snap's confined environment "
"when a certain action occurs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:12
msgid "Common examples of actions requiring hooks include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:14
msgid ""
"**notifying a snap that something has happened**      Example: If a snap has "
"been upgraded, the snap may need to trigger a scripted migration process to "
"port an old data format to the new one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:0
msgid "<br />\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:0
msgid "\\\\\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:17
msgid ""
"**notifying a snap that a specific operation is in progress**      Example: "
"A snap may need to know when a specific interface connects or disconnects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:20
msgid ""
"A hook is defined as an executable within a snap's `meta/hooks/` directory, "
"and consequently, also within `snap/hooks/` when building with *snapcraft*. "
"See [Snapcraft hook support](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/"
"stable/reference/hooks/) for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:22
msgid ""
"The filename of the executable is based on the name of the hook. If this "
"file exists, *snapd* will execute the file when required by that hook's "
"action."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:24
msgid "The following hooks are currently implemented:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:25
msgid "configure hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:26
msgid "default-configure hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:27
msgid "full-disk-encryption hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:28
msgid "gate-auto-refresh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:29
msgid "install hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:30
msgid "install-device hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:31
msgid "interface hooks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:32
msgid "prepare-device hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:33
msgid "prepare-serial hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:34
msgid "pre-refresh hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:35
msgid "post-refresh hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:36
msgid "remove hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:38
msgid ""
"ⓘ **Default shell environment**: A hook script can only assume a POSIX-"
"compliant shell environment for its execution. If your script needs a "
"specific shell, such as *Bash* or *Zsh*, it needs to be explicitly declared "
"within the script's *hashbang* header (`#!/bin/bash`, for example). Your "
"snap also needs to ensure your chosen shell is available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:40
msgid "Accessing resources"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:42
msgid ""
"If a hook requires access to system resources outside of a snap's confined "
"environment, it will need to use {ref}`slots and plugs <how-to-guides-work-"
"with-snaps-connect-interfaces>` via the {ref}`interface <explanation-"
"interfaces-all-about-interfaces>` mechanism to access those resources."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:44
msgid ""
"When using *Snapcraft* to build the snap, the interface definition will go "
"inside [snapcraft.yaml](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/"
"reference/project-file/anatomy-of-snapcraft-yaml/), and the *snapcraft* "
"command create a {ref}`snap.yaml <reference-development-yaml-schemas-the-"
"snap-format>` within the snap to hold the required metadata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:46
msgid ""
"For example, the following excerpt registers an _install_ hook making use of "
"a {ref}`network <interfaces-network-interface>` plug:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:48
msgid ""
"apps:\n"
"    ...\n"
"\n"
"hooks:\n"
"    install:\n"
"        plugs: [network]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:57
msgid ""
"Hooks are called with no parameters. When a hook needs to request or modify "
"information within *snapd*,  they can do so via the *snapctl* tool, which is "
"always available within a snap's environment. See {ref}`Using the snapctl "
"tool <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-use-snapctl>` for further details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:59
msgid "Transactions and rollback"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:61
msgid ""
"A hook is executed as a single transaction, where a transaction object holds "
"all the configuration changes for that hook. These changes are invisible to "
"the running system until the hook completely finishes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/development/supported-snap-hooks.md:63
msgid ""
"This allows for changes to be rolled back or *unset* if errors occur during "
"the execution of a hook. This happens if a non-zero value is returned with "
"either the _configure_ or _default-configure_ hooks, for example, or if an "
"error occurs with any hook involved with an {ref}`interface auto-connection "
"<explanation-interfaces-interface-auto-connection>`."
msgstr ""

